a-to_type=\"unlinked\" data-show_title=\"1\"></a> </li>\n\t\t<li><a href=\"#\" class=\"permalink\" data-permalink_id=\"None\" data-legacy_id=\"61\" data-signature=\"fb7a24ff40773468d0ca327a9b4c4d71\" data-display_type=\"tooltip\" data-link_text=\"\" data-anchor_text=\"\" data-from_id=\"None\" data-from_type=\"core\" data-to_id=\"0\" data-to_type=\"unlinked\" data-show_title=\"1\"></a> </li>\n\t</ul>\n\t</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p> </p>\n\n<p>Special topics seminars may be offered on occasion that would fall under the Applications (CSC 381), Systems (CSC 382), or Theory/Other (CSC 383) category of electives. These courses are eligible electives in the major.</p>\n\n<p>Students can apply at most one independent study course towards their major. Such an independent study course cannot be used to satisfy the Systems or Applications area requirement. Independent study courses related to a senior honors thesis cannot be counted towards the major.</p>\n","shared":false,"status":{"catalog-id":4,"active":true,"searchable":true,"visible":true},"courses":[],"adhocs":[],"sort_order":1,"children":[]},{"id":1461,"catalog-id":4,"legacy-id":11585,"created":"2026-02-24 18:13:05","modified":"2026-04-10 19:08:12","name":"Minor in Computer Science","code":"CS M","description":"<p>The Minor in Computer Science consists of six courses:<br>\n </p>\n\n<ol start=\"1\">\n\t<li><strong>Introductory Programming:</strong><br>\n\t<br>\n\t<a href=\"#\" class=\"permalink\" data-permalink_id=\"None\" data-legacy_id=\"214\" data-signature=\"1e2659b979b4e9c5a78903421ec4da91\" data-display_type=\"tooltip\" data-link_text=\"\" data-anchor_text=\"\" data-from_id=\"None\" data-from_type=\"core\" data-to_id=\"0\" data-to_type=\"unlinked\" data-show_title=\"1\"></a> or<br>\n\t<a href=\"#\" class=\"permalink\" data-permalink_id=\"None\" data-legacy_id=\"56\" data-signature=\"fa511b170ce467538a5270f16a3fd5eb\" data-display_type=\"dynamic\" data-link_text=\"\" data-anchor_text=\"\" data-from_id=\"None\" data-from_type=\"core\" data-to_id=\"0\" data-to_type=\"unlinked\" data-show_title=\"1\"></a> or<br>\n\t<a href=\"#\" class=\"permalink\" data-permalink_id=\"None\" data-legacy_id=\"57\" data-signature=\"c5bd509efced2d0f3a1848c8270289ef\" data-display_type=\"dynamic\" data-link_text=\"\" data-anchor_text=\"\" data-from_id=\"None\" data-from_type=\"core\" data-to_id=\"0\" data-to_type=\"unlinked\" data-show_title=\"1\"></a> or<br>\n\t<a href=\"#\" class=\"permalink\" data-permalink_id=\"None\" data-legacy_id=\"58\" data-signature=\"14a30f14bed625fe65fad91561c9b2f2\" data-display_type=\"dynamic\" data-link_text=\"\" data-anchor_text=\"\" data-from_id=\"None\" data-from_type=\"core\" data-to_id=\"0\" data-to_type=\"unlinked\" data-show_title=\"1\"></a> or<br>\n\tAdvanced placement credit for Computer Science A<br>\n\t </li>\n\t<li><strong>Intermediate Courses:</strong><br>\n\t<a href=\"#\" class=\"permalink\" data-permalink_id=\"None\" data-legacy_id=\"59\" data-signature=\"60ecba207392b67326928623f843ab3f\" data-display_type=\"tooltip\" data-link_text=\"\" data-anchor_text=\"\" data-from_id=\"None\" data-from_type=\"core\" data-to_id=\"0\" data-to_type=\"unlinked\" data-show_title=\"1\"></a> <br>\n\t<a href=\"#\" class=\"permalink\" data-permalink_id=\"None\" data-legacy_id=\"215\" data-signature=\"f0294588a6eae4eed05ebffb892e11ef\" data-display_type=\"tooltip\" data-link_text=\"\" data-anchor_text=\"\" data-from_id=\"None\" data-from_type=\"core\" data-to_id=\"0\" data-to_type=\"unlinked\" data-show_title=\"1\"></a> <br>\n\t<a href=\"#\" class=\"permalink\" data-permalink_id=\"None\" data-legacy_id=\"689\" data-signature=\"ee9a9b65d72a4fbb98f9a47bc1264356\" data-display_type=\"tooltip\" data-link_text=\"\" data-anchor_text=\"\" data-from_id=\"None\" data-from_type=\"core\" data-to_id=\"0\" data-to_type=\"unlinked\" data-show_title=\"1\"></a> \n\t<p style=\"margin-left:40px;\"> </p>\n\t</li>\n\t<li><strong>Two Electives:<br>\n\tChosen from 300-level CSC courses and MAT courses listed earlier as potential electives in the CSC major.</strong></li>\n</ol>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left:40px;\"> </p>\n\n<p>Special topics seminars may be offered on occasion that would fall under the Applications (CSC 381), Systems (CSC 382), or Theory/Other (CSC 383) category of electives. These courses are eligible electives in the minor.</p>\n\n<p>Unless a specific exception is approved by the department, independent study courses may not be applied towards the CSC minor.</p>\n","shared":false,"status":{"catalog-id":4,"active":true,"searchable":true,"visible":true},"courses":[],"adhocs":[],"sort_order":2,"children":[]},{"id":1496,"catalog-id":4,"legacy-id":11620,"created":"2026-02-24 18:13:05","modified":"2026-04-10 15:08:49","name":"Graduate Studies in Computer Science","code":"CSC","description":"<p>Students considering graduate school in computer science should include MAT 150 and CSC 324 in their coursework and seek opportunities to engage in research. The Graduate Record Examination should be taken during the fall semester of the senior year.</p>\n","shared":false,"status":{"catalog-id":4,"active":true,"searchable":true,"visible":true},"courses":[],"adhocs":[],"sort_order":3,"children":[]},{"id":1497,"catalog-id":4,"legacy-id":11621,"created":"2026-02-24 18:13:05","modified":"2026-04-10 15:09:25","name":"Honors in Computer Science","code":"CSC","description":"<p>The Department of Mathematics and Computer Science grants honors to graduating students with outstanding records of accomplishment in computer science, as demonstrated in three areas: breadth and depth of curriculum, quality of academic performance, and significance of a scholarly project. Breadth and depth of curriculum are achieved by taking MAT 150, two major-eligible electives in the Applications category, two in the Systems category, one in the Theory category, and one more in either the Theory or Other category. Additionally, students will undertake two independent study courses with the honors thesis supervisor for work on the honors project itself.</p>\n\n<p>All candidates for honors must prepare an honors thesis and defend the thesis orally before faculty in the department. Candidates must attain a grade point average of at least 3.2 overall and at least 3.5 on all courses that fulfill either the requirements of the Computer Science major or the course requirements for honors noted above. The final recommendation of the Department for graduation with honors is determined by the quality of the candidate's complete academic record, thesis, and defense. Additionally, the department may choose to confer high honors upon a candidate who displays unusual independence and initiative, develops original results, and clearly and rigorously communicates these via a high-quality thesis and oral defense.</p>\n\n<p>The Department recommends that students interested in an honors program notify their academic advisers and the chair of the department during the spring semester of the sophomore year, or as soon as possible thereafter. During the junior year, such a student should identify an area of computer science for exploration and seek out a member of the department to serve as a potential honors supervisor. Formal declaration<br>\nof pursuit of honors is recommended by the end of the advising period in the spring of the junior year and is due by the end of the first week of classes of the senior year. See the departmental honors page for the appropriate form and further details on the honors process and requirements.</p>\n","shared":false,"status":{"catalog-id":4,"active":true,"searchable":true,"visible":true},"courses":[],"adhocs":[],"sort_order":4,"children":[]},{"id":1462,"catalog-id":4,"legacy-id":11586,"created":"2026-02-24 18:13:05","modified":"2026-09-15 15:03:36","name":"Computer Science Courses","code":"CS C","description":"<p>The 100-level courses are introductory and are recommended for students with no prior experience or coursework in computer science. The 200-level courses are either intermediate courses in fundamental computer science or interdisciplinary courses. The 300-level courses cover advanced topics in computer science, and typically require one or more 200-level courses as prerequisites. The 400-level courses involve more specialized topics.</p>\n","shared":false,"status":{"catalog-id":4,"active":true,"searchable":true,"visible":true},"courses":[{"id":1211,"legacy-id":56986,"catalog-id":4,"title":"BIO 209 - Bioinformatics Programming (= CSC 209)  ","status":{"catalog-id":4,"active":true,"searchable":true,"visible":true},"course_types":[{"id":176,"legacy-id":1426,"catalog-id":4,"created":"2026-02-24 13:13:03","modified":"2026-02-24 13:13:03","name":"Biology","description":"Biology","category":"course","display":false,"order":6,"status":{"catalog-id":4,"active":true,"searchable":true,"visible":true}}],"url":"/api/davidson/catalog/4/course/1211/","sort_order":1},{"id":5569,"legacy-id":58145,"catalog-id":4,"title":"CSC 108 - Exploring Computer Science  ","status":{"catalog-id":4,"active":true,"searchable":true,"visible":true},"course_types":[{"id":182,"legacy-id":1432,"catalog-id":4,"created":"2026-02-24 13:13:03","modified":"2026-02-24 13:13:03","name":"Computer Science","description":"Computer Science","category":"course","display":false,"order":12,"status":{"catalog-id":4,"active":true,"searchable":true,"visible":true}}],"url":"/api/davidson/catalog/4/course/5569/","sort_order":2},{"id":8423,"legacy-id":58798,"catalog-id":4,"title":"CSC 110 - Data Science and Society  ","status":{"catalog-id":4,"active":true,"searchable":true,"visible":true},"course_types":[{"id":182,"legacy-id":1432,"catalog-id":4,"created":"2026-02-24 13:13:03","modified":"2026-02-24 13:13:03","name":"Computer Science","description":"Computer Science","category":"course","display":false,"order":12,"status":{"catalog-id":4,"active":true,"searchable":true,"visible":true}}],"url":"/api/davidson/catalog/4/course/8423/","sort_order":4},{"id":